all of us in the concession community  feel like we're the victim of pretty  high stakes politics  I have been in the park and recreation  business for roughly 40 years and I've  run this company for 33 years our  company currently manages parks for city  agencies counties state and federal but  by far the largest portion of our  businesses campground concessions the  bulk of those are with US forest service  we do have one with the US Army Corps of  Engineers the federal shutdown has  affected all of the Forest Service  campgrounds but ironically dot the Corps  of Engineers Corps of Engineers  campground has remained open but we have  received direction from the Forest  Service to close all of our Forest  Service campgrounds the Forest Service  was moving to on a track to allow us to  remain open which was logical and they  they were derailed by someone higher and  I I don't think it was within the forest  service i think it was outside the  forest service it could have been within  the agriculture department and if not  the AG department it would have to be in  the White House but yeah I think all of  us in the concession community feel like  we're the victim of pretty high stakes  politics our relationship to the federal  government is were issued what's called  a special use permit essentially they  put all of the responsibility for the  day-to-day operations on the backs of  the private companies like my own and  it's essentially putting the campgrounds  on a user pays basis but we are far more  responsive to the public and their  desires and their needs because we have  to be then the government can be because  the government is insulated from that  and it's not their fault they just are  the national forests are not closed they  remain open and you can do what they  call dispersed camping which is where  you just go out in the woods and set up  anywhere on the National Forest in  what's what's kind of ironic is what  they closed were the developed sites  which were the ones that were staffed  and maintained by concessionaires or if  the Forest Service was doing it by  Forest Service staff during the last  shutdown back in Newt Gingrich's day  the government allowed the concession  campgrounds to remain open because  primarily our relationship is that of a  tenant to a landlord we actually pay the  government we do not receive funds from  the government and in those days the  reservation system was directly  contracted with the concessionaire  community so the concessionaires  actually were able to continue getting  reservations that's changed under the  new system under the new reservation  contract the government has got itself  in the middle of the contract so when  the government shutdown and all those  employees were furloughed it interrupted  the reservation service I personally do  not see any difference between now and  1995 except for the reservation service  they wanted to do it that way and they  went to a multi-agency approach I could  live with that and I could live without  reservations for a short period of time  but what I don't understand is how they  can close us when we're not dependent at  all upon appropriated funds it costs a  lot of money to close down a park and to  keep people out and I don't see the  point of that  you
 

